<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of La Tour d'Auvergne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as La Tour d'Auvergne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The La Tour d'Auvergne were a French noble family named after La Tour in Auvergne. By marriage they acquired the counties of Auvergne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> in the late 14th century, and in 1444 the Viscounty of Turenne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeleine-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Madeleine de La Tour d'Auvergne</a> was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>. Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne married the heiress of the La Marck, becoming prince of Sedan and duke of Bouillon; his son was Marshal Turenne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne.jpg" alt="House of La Tour d'Auvergne" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Emmanuel_Th%C3%A9odose_de_La_Tour_de_Bouillon_(1647-1715).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> appear in documents of the 11th and 12th centuries, but the family's history is clear only from the 13th century, when it held the Lordship of la Tour in the County of Auvergne. It married into other families of the French south, among them Ventadour, La Rochefoucauld and Levis-Mirepoix, and kept close ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> popes; many of its members became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and cardinals, above all after Guy de la Tour married Marthe Rogier of Beaufort, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi">Pope Gregory XI</a>, in 1352.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The senior line ended in 1501 with Jean III de la Tour d'Auvergne, the last medieval Count of Auvergne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> and Lauraguais. His elder daughter Anne married John Stewart, 2nd Duke of Albany, and died childless, so both counties passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a>, later Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The junior line descended from Bertrand de La Tour d'Auvergne, lord of Olliergues, who died in 1329. Henry de la Tour d'Auvergne (1555–1623), a Marshal of France, was a faithful supporter of Henri IV's Huguenot cause.</p><p>His son Frederic Maurice, compromised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-coiffier-de-ruze-marquis-of-cinq-mars">Cinq-Mars</a> conspiracy, was pardoned on condition that he give up the strategically important principalities of Sedan, Jametz and Raucourt in exchange for the French dukedoms of Albret and Château-Thierry, an exchange formalised in 1651. In the same year the family became Foreign Princes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His brother, Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, vicomte de Turenne (1611–1675), was buried at Saint-Denis by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. Godefroy Maurice became sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-maurice-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Duke of Bouillon</a> in 1678, when the duchy was retaken from the Spaniards, and Grand Chamberlain of France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The 6th Duke, Godefroy Charles Henri, served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> and nearly ruined the family by spending almost a million livres on a mistress in three months. The duchy was annexed by the French Republic within three years of his death, and the main line ended in 1802 when his only son died without issue. His Hôtel d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, bequeathed to the Empress Josephine, became the Élysée Palace. The duke had adopted Philippe d'Auvergne, a British naval officer, as a possible heir, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave the duchy to the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of La Tour d'Auvergne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The remaining county, centred on Vic-le-Comte, passed to the <b>La Tour</b> family and through Madeleine de la Tour d'Auvergne to her daughter Catherine de' Medici.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-auvergne">County of Auvergne</a></li><li><blockquote>The county passed through the houses of Auvergne and <b>La Tour</b> before the Burgundian State took it in 1435.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li><li><blockquote>Possession was contested between the La Marcks, their <b>La Tour d'Auvergne</b> heirs and Liège.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bouillon">Duchy of Bouillon</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1444 the viscounty passed to the <b>La Tour d'Auvergne</b> family.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-turenne">Viscounty of Turenne</a></li></ul></section>
